Description: Quit an application without a quit action with SIGTERM to the processes of its windows.
 Quit in the dash, the app grid, Ubuntu Dock and the global menu extension calls shell_app_request_quit(), which
 activates the "quit" action of an application which exports one over D-Bus, as GtkApplication does. For any other
 application it closes the windows one by one, and Chromium, Firefox and Electron applications export no such action.
 A browser treats windows closed one by one as windows the user closed: the next start of Chromium restores only the
 window closed last, and Firefox restores at most browser.sessionstore.max_windows_undo of them (Mozilla bug 1827994).
 .
 With this patch, such an application receives SIGTERM instead, to each process which owns one of its windows, once,
 as at the end of the session. Chromium handles it as the end of the session and restores every window at its next
 start, and Firefox does so with patches/firefox/sigterm-handler-after-app-shell.patch (Mozilla bug 2086801). A window
 whose process is not known, or which cannot receive the signal, is still closed, and so are the windows of a
 window-backed application, one which GNOME Shell matched to no desktop file: their process can be a proxy, such as ssh
 for windows forwarded with ssh -X, or waypipe, and SIGTERM would end the connection with every window forwarded through
 it. An application which neither exports a quit action nor handles SIGTERM ends at once, without asking about unsaved
 changes.
Author: Mitar
Bug: https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1827994
Forwarded: not-needed
Last-Update: 2026-11-24

diff --git a/src/shell-app.c b/src/shell-app.c
--- a/src/shell-app.c
+++ b/src/shell-app.c
@@ -2,7 +2,9 @@
 
 #include "config.h"
 
+#include <signal.h>
 #include <string.h>
+#include <unistd.h>
 
 #include <glib/gi18n-lib.h>
 
@@ -1206,6 +1208,11 @@
  * The application may interact with the user, and the user
  * might cancel the quit request from the application UI.
  *
+ * An application which exports a "quit" action receives it. Otherwise
+ * the processes of its windows receive SIGTERM, and its windows whose
+ * process cannot receive it are closed. The windows of a window-backed
+ * application are closed.
+ *
  * This operation may not be supported for all applications.
  *
  * Returns: %TRUE if a quit request is supported for this application
@@ -1214,6 +1221,8 @@
 shell_app_request_quit (ShellApp   *app)
 {
   GActionGroup *group = NULL;
+  g_autoptr (GHashTable) signalled = NULL;
+  gboolean terminate;
   GSList *iter;
 
   if (shell_app_get_state (app) != SHELL_APP_STATE_RUNNING)
@@ -1232,10 +1241,33 @@
       return TRUE;
     }
 
-  /* Otherwise, fall back to closing all the app's windows */
+  /* Otherwise, the processes of the app's windows are asked to quit with
+   * SIGTERM, as at the end of the session. Closing the windows one by one
+   * instead makes Chromium restore only the window closed last, and Firefox
+   * at most browser.sessionstore.max_windows_undo of them. A window whose
+   * process is not known, or cannot receive the signal, is closed.
+   *
+   * The windows of a window-backed app are closed too: the process which
+   * owns them can be a proxy for programs elsewhere, such as ssh for windows
+   * forwarded with ssh -X, or waypipe, and SIGTERM would end the connection
+   * with every window forwarded through it.
+   */
+  terminate = !shell_app_is_window_backed (app);
+  signalled = g_hash_table_new (NULL, NULL);
   for (iter = app->running_state->windows; iter; iter = iter->next)
     {
       MetaWindow *win = iter->data;
+      pid_t pid = meta_window_get_pid (win);
+
+      if (terminate && pid > 0 && pid != getpid ())
+        {
+          gpointer key = GINT_TO_POINTER (pid);
+
+          if (!g_hash_table_contains (signalled, key))
+            g_hash_table_insert (signalled, key, GINT_TO_POINTER (kill (pid, SIGTERM) == 0));
+          if (GPOINTER_TO_INT (g_hash_table_lookup (signalled, key)))
+            continue;
+        }
 
       if (!meta_window_can_close (win))
         continue;
